A||N||18||polomeno||SAE 80/90  in a manual tranny?||||1||
Z||000000||polomeno||11-22-2000||09:10 PM||||Hey guys.. my clutch only seems to shift correctly when i start it up in the cold , and it gets damn cold in canada , after a few mins it wont go into reverse .. i have never changed the tranny fluid myself and i doubt the first owner did.. a mechanic i know suggested i put 80/90 into the transmission and said hte old fluid was probly broken down.. i have a 84 4 cyl 2.5 .. will this fluid work correctly? manual suggests 5w30?<BR>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000001||polomeno||11-23-2000||01:00 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||11-23-2000||01:58 PM||||Don't think 80/90 gear oil is a good idea.  <BR>GM recommended different fluids in their transaxles over the years, but never recommended 80/90 (oops - except for the Corvair, but that's a different story).  Think gear oil, being thicker, would tend to give you <B>more</B> trouble when it's cold.<P>Check the fluid level in the Xaxle, change it if you want, but I'd put in the recommended 5W-30.  It's possible the clutch or shift cable(s) are hanging up in the cold for some reason.<P>Reminds me- (yes, another Stu story, sorry  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">. I'll try keeping it short)  Had a 1982 Cavalier 4-speed; had freezing rain one day, and it wouldn't shift out of 1st gear until about 1/3 of the way home.  Was that ever a slow trip! After things warmed up, it shifted normally.  One cable had a cracked boot- sprayed cable with WD-40, never had the problem again.||||reg||4
Z||000003||Cooter||11-23-2000||02:21 PM||||I used Mobil-1 0w30 in my Isuzu 5 speed last winter. It worked really good on those cold mornings. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Eric_PA||11-23-2000||02:34 PM||||I wouldn't reccomend using the 80/90 in your trans. I have an 84 4 speed and did put that in because the guys at the local auto parts store told me that i should use it. The trans would not work right for me, it actually slowed the car down noticeably. I ended up putting in the 5W-30 recomended by the manual and have no problems with it now.<P>Eric<BR>Black 86.5 Fastbasck<BR>red 84 winter car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000005||theogre||11-23-2000||03:38 PM||||80W-90 "gear" oil is NOT made for the type gears put into the GM FWD transmissions. NO GM FWD tranny I know of uses it. You can damage the tranny by putting it in there. Anyone telling you otherwise is incompetant. (Some FWD manual trannys even use ATF.)<P>Gear oil is made for the type gears used in the diferantial of traditional RWD cars. The regular stuff is a high sulfer oil that can damage things not made to use it. (Never ever use it for motor oil!)||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||11-23-2000||10:00 PM||||I pulled the following quote from a Fiero mail list where a heavy discussion on trans lube is currently going on.  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Personal Preference smacks of an opinion based on faith.  I use Valvoline<BR>70w90 synthetic GEAR LUBE cause it works.  I tried GM's other recommendations<BR>through the years and decided to try something similar to their formulation<BR>which did not require a visit to the dealership.  I have been trying to convince<BR>another reader of the dynamics of a synthetic.  Your choice and determination<BR>of the 5w30 points out that fact regarding synthetics.  Red Line makes a synthetic<BR>gear lube for transaxles which is fairly high in SAE viscosity.  I suspect your<BR>choice<BR>works well in your climate.  I live in Southern California and spend much time<BR>at 3200+ rpm in 5th gear.  The heat and shifting stress are nicely tamed by the<BR>"heavier" gear lube.  Let each person follow their faith or their science.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have in the past used 80w90 gear oil in a 5spd Isuzu for about three years (little use)with no problems. If a manual transaxle fails, I doubt the type of oil had much to do with it, but rather it was just worn out. The type of oil can have a drastic affect on the way it shifts though.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||11-24-2000||12:45 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>...If a manual transaxle fails, I doubt the type of oil had much to do with it, but rather it was just worn out...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, guess what you put lube in a manual transaxle for?  Wear, maybe   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?  Fine. I'm not about to argue this point.  Chances are many types of oil will work, and I guess I can't argue with three years of little use.  <BR>Actually, rockcrawl, I appreciate your input.  Thanks   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<P>Probably the reason subjects like this (e.g. - clutch hydraulic fluid, trans. lube, etc.) never get resolved here, is that it takes a <B>long</B> time (or, an accelerated simulation) to determine the effects of long-term use.  Most of us don't have the time or facilities, and don't want to risk an engine or transmission to find out on our own.   <P>I am <B>sure</B> that GM has done a lot more research and has lots more data than I do, though.  That's why I stick with their recommended lube.<P>BTW, Ogre - high sulfur content, eh?  Guess that explains the smell of that stuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">: ! Could never get rid of that smell in my jeans... other than throwing the jeans away  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!<BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 11-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||11-24-2000||02:26 PM||||there aren't to many things involved with a car that smell worse..... Maybe some of the citrus based cleaners would get rid of it. Those work well for allot of stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000009||rockcrawl||11-24-2000||05:09 PM||||Stu, What I meant is that most Fieros have high mileage by now, and most of the ones that are failing are not failing because the owner just switched to a differnt type of oil, but rather they are failing because they have years of wear. There is nothing you could dump into a worn out transaxle that will make it work like a new one, if it's shot, it's shot. I just think there are a lot of people who blame a destroyed trans on the oil type, when in fact it was just normal wear (compounded over the life of the car) or abuse.<P> I don't support the use of anything other than what GM recommends. I used the gear oil because I was too lazy to look at the manual to see what I was supposed to use. It has since been changed to 5w30. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||StuGood||11-24-2000||06:24 PM||||Makes sense to me, RC  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.    ||||reg||14
Z||000011||polomeno||11-24-2000||06:50 PM||||Yes.. but what about me... my clutch works fine until the engin is heated up?? that's why I was thinking that it was the tranny oil? I understand that if its' shot its' shot , but there is surely no such thing as having it only shot when its' hot ??<P>thanks guys<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000012||StuGood||11-24-2000||07:05 PM||||Is going into reverse when warm the only problem, or does it have trouble going into all gears?||||reg||5
Z||000013||polomeno||11-24-2000||07:11 PM||||nope the rest of the gears are fine 1st is a bit stiff but i dont have a problem with it .. reverse goes in fine for about 5-10 minutes when is start it when its' cold.. in the summer reverse would not work at all.. now that it's -20 reverse works till it warms up?<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000014||StuGood||11-24-2000||07:27 PM||||So does it "buzz" when you try to put it into reverse?  Have you tried the trick of putting it into 1st or 2nd gear first, and <B>then</B> trying to get it into reverse?<P>Only thing I know of that the cold would do, to allow it to go into gear, is thicken the lube so the gears stop their rotation sooner.<BR>That, or the clutch is hanging up (not releasing) when warm for some reason.<P>BTW, I apologize if these questions sound  stupid - just trying to figure out what's going on  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   ||||reg||1
Z||000015||polomeno||11-24-2000||07:40 PM||||ya i do try putting it into 1st or 2nd then into reverse and it just doesnt go after a few minutes.. i can hear it grinding the gears if i try ... that's why i was asking about possible thicker oil in the tranny cause the cold causing it to be thicker is the only thing i can think of.. also it has probly never been changed and it has 176 000 km on it so it could just be broken down too much??<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000016||2xsess||11-24-2000||09:02 PM||||I know this may sound strange but I had the same trouble with my 85Gt 4spd. The clutch would work great until about 5 minutes of driving, it turned out to be the slave cylinder. <P>Don't know if that'll help<P>Jeff<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||polomeno||11-24-2000||09:16 PM||||ya i had thought of that to begin with so the master and slave cylinder are both new along with a new steel clutch pedal<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000018||StuGood||11-25-2000||11:00 AM||||Well, just to tell you what you probably already figured out by now, since it grinds going into reverse (assuming car is stationary, and you stopped the gears from spinning by putting it into another gear before trying reverse), the problem boils down to: The clutch isn't releasing fully when the car is warmed up.  The "goes into 1st with difficulty" supports this also.<BR>At this point, I doubt tranny lube has anything to do with it.<BR> <BR>If the linkage isn't binding when hot, and if the clutch hydraulics have been properly bled, you'll have to go inside the clutch, I think  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.<P>Could be some weird deal like crank end play, I suppose, but that really doesn't make sense to me, either.  In either case, you'd still have to take the clutch apart to figure out what's going on.<P><BR>||||reg||1
